From 43b3b91aa5423902fc80f6702936d10f69fce82b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 21:38:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: an output of Kodi's device whose sound comes
 into use is lit again with a change of mode

When the audio output in use moves (the user chooses another one) to an output
of the graphics card that Kodi draws on, which Kodi turned off when it came up,
or when the output that Kodi leaves in a move goes off although its sound is in
use, that output is lit again. Not in a commit of its own: lighting another CRTC
is a mode set, which can change the mode of Kodi's own output too where the two
share a clock or the bandwidth of the device. It goes in the next commit that
changes a mode anyway (a video that starts or ends in another mode, any mode set
of Kodi's own CRTC), never in one of native Dolby Vision, nor in a move
(CDRMAtomic::BringBack()).

- Its CRTC is the one it had where that is free (not the one in use, not on, not
  named by another output), else the first free one that can drive it; its mode
  the one it had when Kodi turned it off (kept then: m_offOutputs), else the one
  its display prefers (KEEP_OUTPUTS::BringBackCrtc()).
- The whole commit, with it, is asked of the driver first (TEST_ONLY, on a copy
  of the request), in this order (KEEP_OUTPUTS::BringBackWays()): black with no
  plane on it, where the driver keeps a CRTC on so (not where it needs a primary
  plane); then with a black picture of its own on a free primary plane of that
  CRTC (amdgpu): a dumb buffer of the size of the mode, written black by Kodi,
  freed when Kodi ends. The first way that the driver takes goes with the commit.
- Where the driver takes the commit only without it, it stays off and is not asked
  again: its sound comes back when Kodi starts again. Where it does not take the
  commit even without it, nothing is known of it, and it is asked again with the
  next one (KEEP_OUTPUTS::BringBackVerdict()). A commit that carried it and was
  refused all the same leaves it off, said so.
- Until it is lit, the user is told once, with a notification (string #41010,
  translated in Estuary as #32440, Spanish included): "The sound of <display>
  comes back with the next video that changes the display mode, or after
  restarting Kodi".
- Without atomic modesetting nothing of this is done; the log says that it is on
  again when Kodi starts again, as the commit before this one did on any driver.

kodi.log, at info level unless a warning is said:
  "QueueSoundBringBack: /dev/dri/card0: HDMI-A-2 carries the sound in use, but it
   was turned off when Kodi came up: it is lit again in the next commit that
   changes a display mode anyway (a video that changes it), or when Kodi starts
   again; the user is told"
  "BringBack: HDMI-A-2 carries the sound in use: it is lit again in this change of
   mode, on CRTC <id> in 3840x2160 @ 60 Hz, black, with nothing on it"
   (or "..., black, with a black picture of its own")
  "DrmAtomicCommit: HDMI-A-2 lit again, for the sound in use, with this change of
   mode"
  warning "BringBack: HDMI-A-2 carries the sound in use, but the driver takes this
   change of mode only without it lit again (...): it stays off; its sound comes
   back when Kodi starts again"
  warning "BringBack: HDMI-A-2 carries the sound in use, but no CRTC that is off
   can drive it: it stays off; its sound comes back when Kodi starts again"
  warning "DrmAtomicCommit: the change of mode that lit HDMI-A-2 again for the
   sound in use was refused (...): it stays off; its sound comes back when Kodi
   starts again"

Tested on the host: the order of the ways, the choice of the CRTC and the verdict
(100 checks with those of the commits before; twenty-one mutations of the header,
each caught). The commit itself, the dumb buffer and the notification are read
and syntax-checked against the build's own compile commands; what each driver
takes, and whether the mode of Kodi's own output changes for an instant when the
other CRTC is lit, only hardware shows.
